derivations.claim:
By [F3] and [F4], smooth boundary data yield solutions smooth up to $\partial M$. For smooth test data $h$, choose a smooth collar extension $V_h$ by [F8]. In a boundary chart, write $\operatorname{div}_g(\gamma
abla_gv)=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_i(|g|^{1/2}\gamma g^{ij}\partial_jv)$ and $\Delta_gu=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_i(|g|^{1/2}g^{ij}\partial_ju)$. Apply the Euclidean Gauss-Green identity [F5] to the coordinate fluxes; summing over the finite partition gives the Riemannian boundary flux, while artificial chart boundaries cancel and the density transforms to $dS_g$. For the conductivity equation it yields $$\langle\Lambda_\gamma f,hangle=\int_{\partial M}\gamma\,\partial_{
u_{m out}}v_f\,\overline h\,dS_g,$$ since $\operatorname{div}_g(\gamma
abla_gv_f)=0$. For the Schrödinger equation it yields $$\langle\Lambda_qf,hangle=\int_{\partial M}\partial_{
u_{m out}}u_f\,\overline h\,dS_g,$$ since $(-\Delta_g+q)u_f=0$. The extensions required for the weak pairings exist by [F6], and smooth test extensions exist by [F8].
